# Migrations must round-trip byte-for-byte, for two reasons.
#
# V2 carries real data, and 86 of its lines sit inside multi-line string literals
# — a company name that spans two lines, a contact note that spans three. On a
# Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) git would rewrite those LFs to CRLF and
# the CR would land inside the restored column value, exactly as it would in a
# COPY block. Same hazard the dumps in db-export/ are protected from.
#
# And Flyway records a checksum per migration. A file whose bytes differ between
# a Windows checkout and a Linux one is a file that fails validate-on-migrate in
# whichever environment did not apply it first — the app then refuses to start,
# with a checksum mismatch on a migration nobody edited.
*.sql -text
